  "query_preview": "..\n\n-  Winter Weather Advisories continue for central Idaho, the\n   Bitterroot/Sapphire Mountains, and Southwest Montana for heavy\n   mountain snow and slick valley travel.\n\n-  A stalled frontal band will transition valley rain to snow \n   along the US-93 corridor this morning, leaving up to one inch \n   of slushy accumulation.\n\n-  Widespread freeze-thaw cycles will create patchy ice on valley\n   roadways tonight into Wednesday morning.\n\nA slow-moving winter system is currently pushing a stalled \nfrontal deformation band across West-Central Montana and central \nIdaho early this morning. Breezy west-northwest winds are \naccompanying ongoing mountain snow and valley rain. As colder air \ncontinues to filter in behind the front, snow levels will drop\ntoward valley floors around sunrise. This will transition ongoing\nprecipitation to a rain/snow mix or all snow along the US-93 \ncorridor, including the Bitterroot Valley, Missoula, and Seeley- \nSwan. Valleys along US-93 can expect a trace to one inch of slushy\nsnow accumulation by mid-morning, with minor valley impacts \nextending north toward the Flathead Valley and up to Marias Pass.\n\nBy this afternoon, precipitation will taper across West-Central \nMontana. The heaviest snowfall rates will pivot south and east \ninto Lemhi County and Southwest Montana, heavily impacting Butte, \nHomestake Pass, and Georgetown Lake. Winter Weather Advisories \nremain active for these higher terrain areas where significant, \nplowable snow accumulations are likely to cause difficult travel.\n\nWhile lower elevation roads will remain mostly wet and slushy \nduring the daytime hours, plummeting temperatures tonight will \nresult in a widespread freeze-thaw hazard. Patchy ice is expected \nto form on wet road surfaces, with bridges and overpasses being \nparticularly vulnerable into Wednesday morning.\n\nA secondary trough will drop into the region Thursday into Friday.\nThis feature will maintain colder ambient temperatures and bring\nadditional accumulating mountain snow, alongside a rain/snow mix\nfor valleys primarily south of the I-90 corridor.",
